1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:10,333
[Recording: Perseus black hole — NASA Chandra sonification]

2
00:00:11,466 --> 00:00:13,198
That is a black hole.

3
00:00:13,198 --> 00:00:19,778
A real one, at the center of the Perseus galaxy cluster, two hundred fifty million light years from Earth.

4
00:00:19,778 --> 00:00:23,587
What you just heard is real data from NASA's Chandra observatory.

5
00:00:24,733 --> 00:00:29,449
For seventy years we have been recording space. Most of it is silence.

6
00:00:29,449 --> 00:00:34,529
But every now and then, our machines pick up something that makes scientists stop.

7
00:00:35,666 --> 00:00:40,539
These are the fifteen scariest real sounds ever recorded in space, ranked.

8
00:00:40,539 --> 00:00:43,788
Every single clip is genuine, sourced, and labeled.

9
00:00:43,788 --> 00:00:49,067
And the sound at number one made NASA's own team call it spooky.

10
00:00:50,199 --> 00:00:56,625
Before we start, one honest rule. Space is a vacuum. Sound cannot travel through it.

11
00:00:57,766 --> 00:01:06,684
So when a spacecraft hears something, it is recording radio waves, plasma waves, or vibrations — real physical signals, converted into audio.

12
00:01:06,684 --> 00:01:13,170
On this channel, every clip gets a label telling you exactly what it is. No fakes.

13
00:01:13,170 --> 00:01:20,466
No sound effects. If a famous space sound could not be verified, it is not in this video.

14
00:01:21,599 --> 00:01:25,230
Number fifteen. The beep that terrified a superpower.

15
00:01:26,366 --> 00:01:31,319
October fourth, nineteen fifty seven. The Soviet Union puts the first machine into orbit.

16
00:01:31,319 --> 00:01:37,333
Anyone on Earth with a ham radio can hear it passing over their house. Twice a second.

17
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:40,870
For twenty two days. America does not find it cute.

18
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:47,238
It finds it terrifying — because if a radio can fly over your country, so can a warhead.

19
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:52,758
[Recording: Sputnik 1 radio transmission, received 1957]

20
00:01:53,899 --> 00:01:57,608
Number fourteen. The sound of stars dying.

21
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:05,150
The Kepler telescope watched stars flicker as they swell into red giants near the end of their lives.

22
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:14,776
Convert those flickers into audio, and dying stars sound like this — slow organ notes, each one the resonant frequency of a star running out of fuel.

23
00:02:14,776 --> 00:02:17,985
One day, the Sun will sing this same note.

24
00:02:19,133 --> 00:02:34,133
[Sonification: Kepler starquakes of a dying red giant]

25
00:02:35,266 --> 00:02:39,942
Number thirteen. The first sound ever recorded on another planet.

26
00:02:41,066 --> 00:02:42,584
February twenty twenty one.

27
00:02:42,584 --> 00:02:49,413
The Perseverance rover switches on its microphone — the first working mic on the surface of another planet.

28
00:02:49,413 --> 00:02:55,104
And Mars speaks. Thin, cold wind, in an atmosphere one percent as thick as ours.

29
00:02:55,104 --> 00:02:57,760
This is not data converted to sound.

30
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:04,968
This is a real microphone, really recording, on a world where nothing has ever been alive to hear it.

31
00:03:06,099 --> 00:03:26,162
[Recording: Martian wind — Perseverance SuperCam microphone, Sol 1]

32
00:03:27,299 --> 00:03:31,818
Number twelve. A robot, alone, shooting rocks with a laser.

33
00:03:32,966 --> 00:03:38,373
That same rover carries a laser that vaporizes rock to study what Mars is made of.

34
00:03:38,373 --> 00:03:41,752
On Sol twelve, the microphone caught it firing thirty times.

35
00:03:41,752 --> 00:03:48,848
Each snap you hear is stone turning to plasma, on a planet with no one around for two hundred million kilometers.

36
00:03:49,966 --> 00:04:01,042
[Recording: SuperCam laser zapping Martian rock, Sol 12]

37
00:04:02,166 --> 00:04:06,032
Number eleven. The loneliest drive in the universe.

38
00:04:07,166 --> 00:04:13,297
When Perseverance drives, its metal wheels grind over rock in the Martian cold, and the whole frame groans.

39
00:04:13,297 --> 00:04:19,428
NASA engineers admitted they would take the car to the shop if it made these sounds on Earth.

40
00:04:19,428 --> 00:04:24,877
Listen to a one ton machine, crawling across an empty planet, creaking like a haunted house.

41
00:04:25,999 --> 00:04:55,999
[Recording: Perseverance driving across Jezero Crater, Sol 16]

42
00:04:57,133 --> 00:05:00,294
Number ten. The moon that whispers to its planet.

43
00:05:01,433 --> 00:05:06,266
Enceladus is a tiny ice moon that vents its hidden ocean into space.

44
00:05:06,266 --> 00:05:11,471
Those particles ride Saturn's magnetic field lines — and as they do, they hiss.

45
00:05:11,471 --> 00:05:17,420
Cassini flew through it and recorded an ocean world, electrically whispering to the planet it orbits.

46
00:05:18,566 --> 00:05:29,247
[Spacecraft data: Enceladus plasma hiss — Cassini]

47
00:05:30,366 --> 00:05:33,057
Number nine. Alien lightning.

48
00:05:34,199 --> 00:05:35,366
Nineteen seventy nine.

49
00:05:35,366 --> 00:05:41,590
Voyager one reaches Jupiter and proves, for the first time, that lightning exists on another world.

50
00:05:41,590 --> 00:05:52,093
The storm's radio waves spiral down Jupiter's magnetic field and arrive as falling whistles — like something calling down from clouds big enough to swallow the Earth.

51
00:05:53,233 --> 00:06:01,053
[Spacecraft data: Jupiter lightning whistlers — Voyager 1]

52
00:06:02,199 --> 00:06:05,752
Number eight. Crashing into a monster's territory.

53
00:06:06,899 --> 00:06:10,709
Jupiter's magnetic field is the largest structure in the solar system.

54
00:06:10,709 --> 00:06:22,485
In twenty sixteen, the Juno spacecraft slammed through its outer boundary — the bow shock — where the solar wind piles up against the planet's magnetosphere like water against the bow of a ship.

55
00:06:22,485 --> 00:06:26,987
Juno crossed it in two hours. This is what the crossing sounded like.

56
00:06:28,133 --> 00:06:44,617
[Spacecraft data: Juno crossing Jupiter's bow shock]

57
00:06:45,766 --> 00:06:49,005
Number seven. The moon that screamed back.

58
00:06:50,133 --> 00:06:52,050
June seventh, twenty twenty one.

59
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:57,418
Juno skims one thousand kilometers over Ganymede, the biggest moon in the solar system.

60
00:06:57,418 --> 00:07:04,320
Its instruments pick up the moon's electric environment — and halfway through the recording, the pitch suddenly jumps.

61
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:10,454
That jump is the exact moment the spacecraft crossed into a different region of Ganymede's magnetosphere.

62
00:07:10,454 --> 00:07:12,755
It sounds like the moon noticed.

63
00:07:13,899 --> 00:07:53,899
[Spacecraft data: Juno's Ganymede flyby — listen for the jump]

64
00:07:55,033 --> 00:07:59,265
Number six. The planet that shook — with nobody on it.

65
00:08:00,399 --> 00:08:01,655
April twenty nineteen.

66
00:08:01,655 --> 00:08:08,771
The InSight lander's seismometer, sitting on a planet we thought was geologically dead, feels the ground tremble.

67
00:08:08,771 --> 00:08:10,445
The first confirmed marsquake.

68
00:08:10,445 --> 00:08:16,305
NASA processed the seismic data into audio so we could hear a dead planet...

69
00:08:16,305 --> 00:08:17,979
groan in its sleep.

70
00:08:19,099 --> 00:08:49,099
[Sonification: the first marsquake — InSight seismometer data]

71
00:08:50,233 --> 00:08:53,942
Number five. Falling through the sky of an alien moon.

72
00:08:55,066 --> 00:08:56,905
January fourteenth, two thousand five.

73
00:08:56,905 --> 00:09:05,732
The Huygens probe parachutes for two and a half hours through the orange haze of Titan — the most distant landing in human history.

74
00:09:05,732 --> 00:09:13,088
Its sensors captured the wind of that descent; the European Space Agency reconstructed it from the probe's real atmospheric data.

75
00:09:13,088 --> 00:09:18,237
A parachute, an alien sky, and a machine falling alone toward a methane world.

76
00:09:19,366 --> 00:09:49,366
[Reconstruction from real Huygens descent data — Titan's wind]

77
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:56,378
And as the ground rose to meet it, Huygens' radar pinged faster... and faster.

78
00:09:56,378 --> 00:10:01,836
This is the sonified radar echo of the surface of Titan, rushing up.

79
00:10:02,966 --> 00:10:27,966
[Sonification: Huygens radar altimeter — surface approaching]

80
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:34,206
Before the top four — the scariest space signal ever detected is NOT in this ranking.

81
00:10:34,206 --> 00:10:36,440
And the reason why should bother you.

82
00:10:37,566 --> 00:10:39,601
August fifteenth, nineteen seventy seven.

83
00:10:39,601 --> 00:10:50,184
The Big Ear telescope in Ohio catches a narrowband radio burst from the direction of Sagittarius — seventy two seconds, thirty times louder than the background.

84
00:10:50,184 --> 00:10:55,068
The astronomer on duty circles the printout and writes one word. Wow.

85
00:10:56,200 --> 00:11:00,227
Here is the part nobody tells you. There is no recording.

86
00:11:00,227 --> 00:11:04,255
The Wow signal existed only as intensity numbers on a printout.

87
00:11:04,255 --> 00:11:09,016
Every Wow signal audio you have ever heard on the internet is fabricated.

88
00:11:09,016 --> 00:11:14,508
It never repeated, and it has never been fully explained. The scariest sound in space...

89
00:11:14,508 --> 00:11:17,072
is one we never got to hear.

90
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:23,110
Number four. Your own planet is singing. You just cannot hear it.

91
00:11:24,233 --> 00:11:28,151
This one is not from deep space. It is from here.

92
00:11:28,151 --> 00:11:37,057
Electrons trapped in Earth's radiation belts generate radio waves every dawn — and played as audio, our planet sounds like a flock of alien birds.

93
00:11:37,057 --> 00:11:40,619
Ham radio operators have picked this up for a century.

94
00:11:40,619 --> 00:11:43,825
You have lived inside this sound your whole life.

95
00:11:44,966 --> 00:12:14,850
[Spacecraft data: Earth's dawn chorus — Van Allen Probes]

96
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,474
Number three. The deepest note in the universe.

97
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:24,260
The sound that opened this video deserves its full story.

98
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:37,072
The black hole at the heart of the Perseus cluster drives pressure waves through the hot gas around it — actual sound waves, in space gas, at B flat, fifty seven octaves below middle C.

99
00:12:37,072 --> 00:12:39,268
One vibration every ten million years.

100
00:12:39,268 --> 00:12:46,223
NASA raised the real waves fifty seven octaves so human ears could stand in front of a black hole.

101
00:12:46,223 --> 00:12:49,152
Listen again — and remember it is real.

102
00:12:50,300 --> 00:13:24,304
[Sonification: Perseus black hole pressure waves, pitched up 57 octaves]

103
00:13:25,433 --> 00:13:28,750
Number two. The loneliest sound ever recorded.

104
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:32,890
Voyager one is the farthest human object in existence.

105
00:13:32,890 --> 00:13:42,857
In twenty twelve, its instruments heard the plasma around it ring at a new, higher tone — and that tone was the proof it had left the Sun's bubble entirely.

106
00:13:42,857 --> 00:13:51,163
This is the sound of interstellar space itself, recorded by a machine that will drift among the stars long after everyone watching this is gone.

107
00:13:52,300 --> 00:14:16,913
[Spacecraft data: interstellar plasma ringing — Voyager 1]

108
00:14:18,033 --> 00:14:32,322
Years earlier, at the edge of the Sun's domain, Voyager heard something bigger: the heliopause booming as shockwaves from solar storms slammed into interstellar gas — among the most powerful radio emissions in the whole solar system, coming from its outermost wall.

109
00:14:33,466 --> 00:14:58,466
[Spacecraft data: heliospheric radio emissions — Voyager]

110
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:03,571
Number one. The sound NASA itself called eerie.

111
00:15:04,700 --> 00:15:15,532
As Cassini approached Saturn in two thousand three, it recorded the planet's auroral radio emissions — and when NASA released the audio, their own caption called it eerie.

112
00:15:15,532 --> 00:15:20,174
Rising and falling shrieks, sweeping tones, sounds that trade places like voices.

113
00:15:20,174 --> 00:15:28,299
These emissions are generated by Saturn's auroras — but their fine structure was so complex that scientists spent years decoding it.

114
00:15:28,299 --> 00:15:32,168
Twenty seven minutes of Saturn, compressed into seventy three seconds.

115
00:15:32,168 --> 00:15:35,262
This is the reigning scariest sound in space.

116
00:15:36,400 --> 00:16:49,893
[Spacecraft data: Saturn kilometric radiation — Cassini, uncut]

117
00:16:51,033 --> 00:16:58,473
Every sound you just heard is real, and every source is linked in the description — the missions, the data, the labels.

118
00:16:58,473 --> 00:17:03,885
That is the deal on this channel: the universe is scary enough without making anything up.

119
00:17:05,033 --> 00:17:14,915
And if hearing a black hole unsettled you, wait until you see what happened to the bodies of every astronaut who died up there — that story is on your screen now.

120
00:17:14,915 --> 00:17:18,930
Subscribe to mindwired, so you do not miss what space sounds like next.

